# scenarios/8_credit_note_and_bad_debt.books: loaded by ledger_server into 8_credit_note_and_bad_debt.db the first time it is picked,
# and again by Start over. Amounts are in cents.
title	8. A credit note and a bad debt: revenue that does not come in
about	A credit note takes back part of a sale: customer A returns damaged goods, so 500 of the sale is cancelled, the arrow going from Trade receivables back to Sales.
about	A bad debt is a sale that stays revenue but is never paid: customer B goes bankrupt, so the 2,000 it owes moves from Trade receivables to Doubtful receivables, and when nothing comes of the bankruptcy it is written off to Bad debts, a cost. (With VAT, the VAT on it could be claimed back too.)
currency	USD
account	100000	equity	Share capital
account	550000	asset	Bank
account	400000	asset	Trade receivables
account	407000	asset	Doubtful receivables
account	700000	revenue	Sales
account	634000	expense	Bad debts
entry	2011-01-02	The founder puts in savings
posting	Share capital	-500000
posting	Bank	500000
entry	2011-02-01	Sale to customer A: 4,000
posting	Sales	-400000
posting	Trade receivables	400000
entry	2011-02-10	Customer A returns damaged goods: a credit note of 500
posting	Trade receivables	-50000
posting	Sales	50000
entry	2011-03-01	Customer A pays the 3,500 it owes
posting	Trade receivables	-350000
posting	Bank	350000
entry	2011-04-01	Sale to customer B: 2,000
posting	Sales	-200000
posting	Trade receivables	200000
entry	2011-10-01	Customer B goes bankrupt: its 2,000 becomes doubtful
posting	Trade receivables	-200000
posting	Doubtful receivables	200000
entry	2011-12-15	The bankruptcy is closed and nothing is paid: the 2,000 is written off
posting	Doubtful receivables	-200000
posting	Bad debts	200000
